UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Dance from different cultures', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs. Examples include Scottish Highland dancing, Irish step dancing, Greek circle dances, Indian classical and folk dances, Samoan and Tongan dances and kapa haka from Aotearoa New Zealand. Each tradition has its own steps, clothing, music and meaning. Some dances are sacred or restricted, so they are only for particular people or occasions. Learn about a cultural dance from a published source, a teacher or a community member who is happy to share it, and name where it comes from. Never mock or change a dance.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose a cultural dance and describe two things you can see (formation, steps, speed), where it is from and one respectful action before learning more.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with their feet. It is from Ireland. A respectful action is to use a source chosen by my teacher and say where the dance comes from.'' It describes elements, names the origin and shows respect.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun. Each dance has its own meaning, and some are only for particular people, so we ask and learn from the right sources.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWith an adult, choose two cultural dances from a library book or approved website. For each write the place it comes from, two things you can see and one question you would ask a person from that community. Do not copy sacred or restricted dances.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs. Examples include Scottish Highland dancing, Irish step dancing, Greek circle dances, Indian classical and folk dances, Samoan and Tongan dances and kapa haka from Aotearoa New Zealand. Each tradition has its own steps, clothing, music and meaning. Some dances are sacred or restricted, so they are only for particular people or occasions. Learn about a cultural dance from a published source, a teacher or a community member who is happy to share it, and name where it comes from. Never mock or change a dance.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: With an adult, choose two cultural dances from a library book or approved website. For each write the place it comes from, two things you can see and one question you would ask a person from that community. Do not copy s Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a cultural dance on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun.. Correction: Strong answer: ''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun. Each dance has its own meaning, and some are only for particular people, so we ask and learn from the right sources. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a cultural dance on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a cultural dance, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a cultural dance with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a cultural dance. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a cultural dance. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with their feet.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson."],"question":"Choose a cultural dance and describe two things you can see (formation, steps, speed), where it is from and one respectful action before learning more. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a cultural dance; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of a tradition and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun.","Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs.","''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with their feet."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a cultural dance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a tradition and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a tradition and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a cultural dance.","Apply a cultural dance to new material and name the a tradition and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun."],"question":"Which response best applies a cultural dance with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on a cultural dance. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun.","''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with their feet.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for a cultural dance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for a cultural dance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a cultural dance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for a cultural dance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun.","Name a tradition and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","With an adult, choose two cultural dances from a library book or approved website."],"question":"Which independent response best applies a cultural dance to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a cultural dance with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a cultural dance on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a cultural dance on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a cultural dance. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a cultural dance, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun.. Correction: Strong answer: ''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w31-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a cultural dance.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use a cultural dance with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a tradition and explain why it matters.","I can practise respect independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a community source needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs.","options":["Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a cultural dance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher. He writes: ''The dancers hold hands in a line that curves into a circle. The steps are in time with the music and the group moves together. It comes from Greece and is often danced at celebrations.'' He names the formation, the time and the origin, and does not guess the meaning of any parts he does not know.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose a cultural dance and describe two things you can see (formation, steps, speed), where it is from and one respectful action before learning more.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with their feet. It is from Ireland. A respectful action is to use a source chosen by my teacher and say where the dance comes from.'' It describes elements, names the origin and shows respect.","explain":"Dance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs. Examples include Scottish Highland dancing, Irish step dancing, Greek circle dances, Indian classical and folk dances, Samoan and Tongan dances and kapa haka from Aotearoa New Zealand. Each tradition has its own steps, clothing, music and meaning. Some dances are sacred or restricted, so they are only for particular people or occasions. Learn about a cultural dance from a published source, a teacher or a community member who is happy to share it, and name where it comes from. Never mock or change a dance.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun. Each dance has its own meaning, and some are only for particular people, so we ask and learn from the right sources. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Describing a cultural dance.","caption":"Describe, then name the place.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Look</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">formation, steps, speed</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Origin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">where the dance is from</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher. He writes: ''The dancers hold hands in Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher. He writes: ''The dancers hold hands in Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a cultural dance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher. He writes: ''The dancers hold hands in a line that curves into a circle. The steps are in time with the music and the group moves together. It comes from Greece and is often danced at celebrations.'' He names the formation, the time and the origin, and does not guess the meaning of any parts he does not know. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Respect for cultural dance.","caption":"Ask and learn.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">use a community source</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Never</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">mock or change the dance</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a cultural dance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun. Each dance has its own meaning, and some are only for particular people, so we ask and learn from the right sources. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher. He writes: ''The dancers hold hands in a line that curves into a circle. The steps are in time with the music and the group moves together. It comes from Greece and is often danced at celebrations.'' He names the formation, the time and the origin, and does not guess the meaning of any parts he does not know.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''In an Irish step dance the dancers keep their arms by their sides and make quick, light steps with their feet. It is from Ireland. A respectful action is to use a source chosen by my teacher and say where the dance comes from.'' It describes elements, names the origin and shows respect.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that all dances from the same culture are the same, or that any dance can be copied for fun. Each dance has its own meaning, and some are only for particular people, so we ask and learn from the right sources. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a cultural dance with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDance is part of every culture and often shows celebrations, stories, work or beliefs. Examples include Scottish Highland dancing, Irish step dancing, Greek circle dances, Indian classical and folk dances, Samoan and Tongan dances and kapa haka from Aotearoa New Zealand. Each tradition has its own steps, clothing, music and meaning. Some dances are sacred or restricted, so they are only for particular people or occasions. Learn about a cultural dance from a published source, a teacher or a community member who is happy to share it, and name where it comes from. Never mock or change a dance. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Dance from different cultures","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Dance: telling stories through movement","week":31,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ravi watches a short video of a Greek circle dance chosen by his teacher. He writes: ''The dancers hold hands in a line that curves into a circle. The steps are in time with the music and the group moves together. It comes from Greece and is often danced at celebrations.'' He names the formation, the time and the origin, and does not guess the meaning of any parts he does not know.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Describing a cultural dance.","caption":"Describe, then name the place.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Look</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">formation, steps, speed</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Origin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">where the dance is from</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Respect for cultural dance.","caption":"Ask and learn.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">use a community source</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Never</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">mock or change the dance</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w31-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
